{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016txEtu3h5CE919yKAUK7NL","type":"tool_result","content":"35\t## 2. Behavior contract\n36\t\n37\t### 2.1 Built-in provider registry (consumed, not owned)\n38\t\n39\tOwned by the config area; restated because trust and credentials depend on\n40\tit exactly.\n41\t\n42\t```\n43\tname        endpoint                      protocols        key variable\n44\topenai      https://api.openai.com/v1     responses, chat  OPENAI_API_KEY\n45\tanthropic   https://api.anthropic.com/v1  anthropic        ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\n46\topenrouter  https://openrouter.ai/api/v1  chat, responses  OPENROUTER_API_KEY\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\tProtocols are listed in registry order (the first is the starter's).\n50\t\n51\t- `lookupProvider(name)`: exact, case-sensitive name match.\n52\t- `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`: the provider that accepts `protocol`\n53\t  and whose endpoint is the same endpoint as `endpoint`. At most one\n54\t  matches.\n55\t- `sameEndpoint(a, b)`: trim both, parse as URLs; false if either fails to\n56\t  parse or has an empty host. Equal when scheme (case-insensitive), host\n57\t  (case-insensitive, including port), path with trailing `/` removed,\n58\t  raw query string, and userinfo all match. So `https://api.openai.com/v1/`\n59\t  is the OpenAI endpoint.\n60\t- A profile's endpoint is its `endpoint` field verbatim when set, else the\n61\t  protocol default: `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` for `anthropic`,\n62\t  `https://api.openai.com/v1` for every other protocol. OpenRouter is never\n63\t  an implicit default.\n64\t- A profile's protocol after `config.load` defaults to `responses` when\n65\t  omitted. The projection always sees the defaulted protocol.\n66\t- Author-route family keys are normalized by `config.load` (trimmed,\n67\t  lowercased, canonical family). The projection sees normalized keys.\n68\t\n69\t### 2.2 Trust projection\n70\t\n71\t`trustProjection(cfg)` produces:\n72\t\n73\t```ts\n74\tinterface TrustedProfile {\n75\t    selector:string      // \"roles.finder\" or\n76\t                         // \"roles.finder.author_routes.<family>\"\n77\t    profile:string       // profile name\n78\t    endpoint:string      // resolved endpoint string (2.1), not normalized\n79\t    protocol:string      // defaulted protocol\n80\t    credentialEnv:string // see below; \"\" when none\n81\t}\n82\tinterface PromptOverride {\n83\t    name:string          // \"prompts.finder_code\" | \"prompts.finder_prose\"\n84\t                         // | \"prompts.verifier\"\n85\t    path:string          // raw string from the config, unresolved\n86\t}\n87\tinterface TrustProjection {\n88\t    profiles:TrustedProfile[]\n89\t    promptOverrides:PromptOverride[]\n90\t}\n91\t```\n92\t\n93\tOrder and contents:\n94\t\n95\t1. For each role name in `cfg.roles`, sorted ascending (in practice\n96\t   `finder`, then `verifier`; `config.load` rejects any other role name):\n97\t   1. the role's `profile`, selector `roles.<role>`;\n98\t   2. then each author route, families sorted ascending, selector\n99\t      `roles.<role>.author_routes.<family>`.\n100\t   A selected profile name not present in `inference_profiles` is skipped\n101\t   (unreachable after `config.load`, which rejects unknown references).\n102\t   Duplicates are kept: the same profile selected twice appears twice.\n103\t   Profiles no role or route selects are not in the projection.\n104\t2. `credentialEnv` of a profile: its `api_key_env` when non-empty; else,\n105\t   when `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` finds a provider, that provider's\n106\t   key variable; else `\"\"`. The role override variable\n107\t   (`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`) is never part of the projection.\n108\t3. Prompt overrides in fixed order `finder_code`, `finder_prose`,\n109\t   `verifier`; included only when the raw config string is non-empty. The\n110\t   path is the raw string from `.roast.json` (not resolved against the\n111\t   config directory), so approvals survive moving the worktree.\n112\t\n113\tThe projection is computed from the loaded config only. Command-line role\n114\toverrides (`--<role>-base-url`, `--<role>-protocol`, ...) are not part of\n115\tit: they are the operator's own choice on the command line, not the\n116\trepository's.\n117\t\n118\t### 2.3 Which configs need approval\n119\t\n120\t`requiresApproval(projection)` is true when:\n121\t\n122\t- `promptOverrides` is non-empty, or\n123\t- any profile is not *standard*. A profile is standard when\n124\t  `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` finds a provider `P` and\n125\t  `credentialEnv === P.keyEnv`.\n126\t\n127\tConsequences (pinned by tests, 5.1): an implicit endpoint with its\n128\tprotocol's provider is standard; a trailing slash on a built-in endpoint is\n129\tstandard; OpenRouter with no `api_key_env` is standard (its `credentialEnv`\n130\tbecomes `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`); any other key variable on a built-in\n131\tendpoint (`MY_KEY`, another provider's key) needs approval; a protocol the\n132\tprovider does not accept at that endpoint (Anthropic endpoint with `chat`,\n133\tOpenAI endpoint with `anthropic`) needs approval; any custom endpoint needs\n134\tapproval, with or without a key variable.\n135\t\n136\t### 2.4 Trust hash v2 (deliberate change)\n137\t\n138\tThe approval hash identifies the projection's security-relevant values.\n139\tSelectors and profile names are excluded: which approved destination a\n140\trole uses, or what a profile is called, does not change where code and\n141\tcredentials go.\n142\t\n143\t**Hash document.** Build this JSON value from the projection:\n144\t\n145\t```json\n146\t{\n147\t  \"destinations\": [\n148\t    {\"credential_env\": \"<string>\", \"endpoint\": \"<string>\",\n149\t     \"protocol\": \"<string>\"}\n150\t  ],\n151\t  \"prompts\": [\n152\t    {\"name\": \"<string>\", \"path\": \"<string>\"}\n153\t  ]\n154\t}\n155\t```\n156\t\n157\t- `destinations`: one entry per profile in the projection, with\n158\t  `endpoint`, `protocol`, `credential_env` copied verbatim (no URL\n159\t  normalization, no trimming). Remove exact duplicates (all three fields\n160\t  equal). Sort ascending by `endpoint`, then `protocol`, then\n161\t  `credential_env`, comparing each string by UTF-16 code units (the\n162\t  JavaScript `<` operator on strings; the same order RFC 8785 uses for\n163\t  keys). Always an array; `[]` when empty.\n164\t- `prompts`: one entry per prompt override, in the projection's fixed\n165\t  order (`prompts.finder_code`, `prompts.finder_prose`,\n166\t  `prompts.verifier`), `name` including the `prompts.` prefix, `path`\n167\t  verbatim. Not deduplicated, not sorted. Always an array; `[]` when empty.\n168\t- No other members. No version field inside the document.\n169\t\n170\t**Canonicalization.** Serialize the document as RFC 8785 (JCS) canonical\n171\tJSON. Because the document contains only objects, arrays, and strings,\n172\tJCS reduces to: object members sorted by key in UTF-16 code-unit order; no\n173\twhitespace anywhere; arrays in the order given above; strings serialized\n174\texactly as ECMAScript `JSON.stringify` serializes a string (escape `\"`,\n175\t`\\`, and U+0000..U+001F using `\\b \\t \\n \\f \\r` or `\\u00xx` lowercase hex;\n176\teverything else, including `/` and non-ASCII, emitted literally). Key\n177\torder for the two object kinds is therefore\n178\t`credential_env, endpoint, protocol` and `name, path`, and the top level\n179\tis `destinations, prompts`. Implement it in-house (about 20 lines); no new\n180\tdependency.\n181\t\n182\t**Digest.** SHA-256 over the UTF-8 bytes of the canonical string. The\n183\tstored and compared form is the string `sha256:` followed by the 64\n184\tlowercase hex digits of the digest.\n185\t\n186\t**Known-answer vectors** (frozen; use as unit-test fixtures):\n187\t\n188\t1. One custom destination and one prompt override.\n189\t   Canonical:\n190\t   `{\"destinations\":[{\"credential_env\":\"GATEWAY_KEY\",\"endpoint\":\"https://gateway.example/v1\",\"protocol\":\"chat\"}],\"prompts\":[{\"name\":\"prompts.finder_code\",\"path\":\"finder-prompt.md\"}]}`\n191\t   Hash:\n192\t   `sha256:2b18773b1f654fbbd02019247674005c4035f3972cfc8552655a09bb2ac57d27`\n193\t2. Two destinations (sorted), no prompts.\n194\t   Canonical:\n195\t   `{\"destinations\":[{\"credential_env\":\"OPENAI_API_KEY\",\"endpoint\":\"https://api.openai.com/v1\",\"protocol\":\"responses\"},{\"credential_env\":\"\",\"endpoint\":\"https://gw.example/v1\",\"protocol\":\"chat\"}],\"prompts\":[]}`\n196\t   Hash:\n197\t   `sha256:7ac3826edbb00b7b8294e6117b0fa3c70fd4e994de107bf41a23324761252d5c`\n198\t\n199\t(Lines above exceed 80 columns because the vectors must be exact.)\n200\t\n201\tProperties that follow and must hold:\n202\t\n203\t- Editing a model, a threshold, a limit, a gate setting, a profile name,\n204\t  or which role points at an already-listed destination keeps the hash.\n205\t- Changing any endpoint, protocol, credential variable, or prompt-override\n206\t  path (including the routed profile's) changes the hash.\n207\t- JSON key order and whitespace in `.roast.json` never affect the hash.\n208\t\n209\t### 2.5 State directory and trust record location\n210\t\n211\t- `stateDir(dir)`: run `git rev-parse --path-format=absolute\n212\t  --git-common-dir` with working directory `dir`; trim the output; result\n213\t  is `<output>/roast`. If git fails (not a repository), the error message\n214\t  is `not inside a git repository`.\n215\t- `trustPath(dir)` is `<stateDir(dir)>/trust.json`; on failure the error is\n216\t  `trust: not inside a git repository`.\n217\t- Linked worktrees resolve to the main repository's common dir, so they\n218\t  share one record.\n219\t- Every trust operation from the CLI passes the Git toplevel of the\n220\t  process working directory (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`), not the\n221\t  directory of a `--config` file.\n222\t\n223\t### 2.6 Trust record file (`trust.json`)\n224\t\n225\tVersion 2 shape, written by the TypeScript implementation:\n226\t\n227\t```json\n228\t{\n229\t  \"version\": 2,\n230\t  \"approved\": [\n231\t    \"sha256:2b18773b1f654fbbd02019247674005c4035f3972cfc8552655a09bb2ac57d27\"\n232\t  ]\n233\t}\n234\t```\n235\t\n236\t- Serialized with 2-space indentation, member order `version`, `approved`,\n237\t  and a trailing newline (`JSON.stringify(rec, null, 2) + \"\\n\"`).\n238\t- `approved` lists hashes in the order they were approved; no duplicates.\n239\t- There is no `untrust` command. Deleting the file withdraws every approval\n240\t  for the clone.\n241\t\n242\t**Reading** (`readTrustRecord(path)`) yields one of:\n243\t\n244\t| file state | result |\n245\t|---|---|\n246\t| absent (`ENOENT`) | `{kind:'current', approved:[]}` |\n247\t| JSON object, `version` is the number `2` | `{kind:'current', approved}` |\n248\t| JSON object, `version` is the number `1` | `{kind:'legacy'}` |\n249\t| anything else | error |\n250\t\n251\t- For version 2: `approved` missing or `null` reads as `[]`; otherwise it\n252\t  must be an array of strings, else error. Unknown top-level members are\n253\t  ignored on read (Go parity) and dropped on the next write.\n254\t- For version 1 (written by the Go implementation): nothing else in the\n255\t  file is read or validated. Its hashes are never compared: the Go hash\n256\t  serialization is not reproduced (ADR-0003).\n257\t- Errors: unreadable file -> `read trust record: <cause>`; invalid JSON or\n258\t  a non-object or wrongly typed `approved` -> `read trust record <path>:\n259\t  <cause>`; `version` missing or any value other than 1 or 2 -> `trust\n260\t  record <path> has unsupported version <v>`. Errors are never treated as\n261\t  \"not approved\"; they propagate (2.8).\n262\t\n263\t**Approved check** (`isApproved(dir, hash)`) returns `'approved'`,\n264\t`'not-approved'`, or `'legacy'` (record is version 1), or throws.\n265\t\n266\t**Approving** (`approve(dir, hash)`):\n267\t\n268\t1. Read the record.\n269\t2. Version 2 with `hash` already present: return without writing.\n270\t3. Version 2 otherwise: append `hash`, write.\n271\t4. Version 1 (legacy): replace the whole file with a version 2 record\n272\t   whose `approved` is `[hash]`. Legacy content is discarded, not\n273\t   migrated.\n274\t5. Absent: write a version 2 record `[hash]`.\n275\t6. A read error (malformed, unsupported version) aborts approval; the file\n276\t   is left untouched and the error propagates (Go parity: the user removes\n277\t   or fixes the file).\n278\t\n279\t**Write procedure** (shared atomic write, 2.12): create the parent\n280\tdirectory with `mkdir -p` mode `0o755` (subject to umask; an existing\n281\tdirectory's mode is not changed), write a temp file `trust-<random>.json`\n282\tin that directory opened with mode `0o600` and exclusive create, close,\n283\trename over `trust.json`, and remove the temp file on any failure. The\n284\tresulting `trust.json` has mode `0o600`. Error prefix on any step:\n285\t`write trust record: <cause>`.\n286\t\n287\t### 2.7 `roast trust` command\n288\t\n289\t```\n290\troast trust [--config <path>] [--yes]\n291\t```\n292\t\n293\t- No positional arguments (extra args are a usage error, exit 2).\n294\t- `--config <path>`: project config file; default is the nearest\n295\t  `.roast.json` between the working directory and the Git toplevel\n296\t  (config area's `loadProjectConfig`). Help text:\n297\t  `project config file (default: nearest .roast.json)`.\n298\t- `--yes`: record approval without asking; stdin is never read.\n299\t- Short help: `Approve the project config's endpoints and credentials`.\n300\t\n301\tSteps, in order:\n302\t\n303\t1. Resolve the Git toplevel of the working directory. Failure -> usage\n304\t   error `not inside a git repository` (exit 2).\n305\t2. Load the project config (missing config -> the config area's usage\n306\t   error naming `npx roast init`; invalid config -> usage error; both\n307\t   exit 2).\n308\t3. Compute the projection and print the trust summary (2.9) to **stdout**.\n309\t4. If `!requiresApproval`: print `Only default provider endpoints and key\n310\t   variables are used; no approval is needed.` to stdout, exit 0. Nothing\n311\t   is written; no state directory is created.\n312\t5. If not `--yes`: ask `Trust this project config? [y/N] ` on **stdout**\n313\t   and read one answer from stdin (2.10). Anything but consent -> usage\n314\t   error `not approved; nothing recorded` (exit 2), nothing written. A\n315\t   stdin read error other than EOF -> that error (exit 4).\n316\t6. `approve(toplevel, hash)`. Failure -> that error (exit 4).\n317\t7. Print `Approved for this clone.` to stdout, exit 0.\n318\t\n319\tThe command prompts even when the hash is already approved (approval is\n320\tidempotent). It does not check whether stdin is a terminal: with\n321\tnon-terminal stdin it reads whatever is there, and EOF is a decline.\n322\t\n323\t### 2.8 `requireTrust` during `review`\n324\t\n325\t`requireTrust(dir, cfg, io)` runs inside `review` (including the default\n326\tbare `roast` command) after the config is loaded, both roles are resolved\n327\t(which may read stored credentials), role flag overrides and the finder\n328\tflip are applied and validated, and the Git toplevel is found; and before\n329\tthe review scope is built and before any network request.\n330\t`dir` is the Git toplevel of the working directory.\n331\t\n332\t1. Compute the projection. If `!requiresApproval` -> continue (nothing\n333\t   read or written).\n334\t2. `isApproved(dir, hash)`:\n335\t   - throws -> the review fails with that error. It is not a usage error,\n336\t     so the exit code is 4 (Go parity: `ExitPartial`).\n337\t   - `'approved'` -> continue.\n338\t   - `'not-approved'` or `'legacy'` -> step 3.\n339\t3. If stdin and stderr are not both terminals (`interactiveTerminals`,\n340\t   2.11): fail with a usage error (exit 2), never reading stdin.\n341\t   - not-approved message: `the project config names endpoints, credential\n342\t     variables, or prompt files that are not approved for this clone;\n343\t     review and approve them with `npx roast trust``\n344\t   - legacy message (deliberate change): states that this clone's\n345\t     approvals were recorded by an older Roast and are no longer\n346\t     accepted, and names `npx roast trust` to approve again. Wording is\n347\t     free; it must name `npx roast trust`.\n348\t4. On terminals: for `'legacy'`, first print one line to stderr saying the\n349\t   existing approvals came from an older Roast. Then print the trust\n350\t   summary (2.9) to **stderr**, ask `Trust this project config? [y/N] `\n351\t   on stderr, read one answer from stdin.\n352\t   - Declined -> usage error `project config not approved; nothing\n353\t     recorded and nothing sent` (exit 2), nothing written.\n354\t   - stdin read error other than EOF -> that error (exit 4).\n355\t   - Consent -> `approve(dir, hash)` (rewrites a legacy file as version\n356\t     2); failure -> that error (exit 4); then print `Approved for this\n357\t     clone.` to stderr and continue the review.\n358\t\n359\tUntrusted is never `ExitFindings` (1), so the pre-commit gate, which\n360\tblocks only on exit 1, warns and allows the commit (gate area).\n361\t\n362\tThere is no environment-variable bypass of trust. CI approves with\n363\t`npx roast trust --yes`.\n364\t\n365\t### 2.9 Trust summary format\n366\t\n367\tShared by `roast trust` (stdout) and the inline prompt (stderr). Every\n368\tinterpolated value passes through `textsafe.line` (escapes C0 controls\n369\tincluding newline and tab, DEL, C1 controls, invalid UTF-8; textsafe\n370\tarea), so config text cannot add lines or terminal escapes.\n371\t\n372\t```\n373\tThis project config sends code and credentials to:\n374\t  <selector>: profile <profile>\n375\t    endpoint:   <endpoint>\n376\t    protocol:   <protocol>\n377\t    credential: <credentialEnv or \"(none named)\">\n378\t  ...one 4-line block per projection profile, in projection order...\n379\tand replaces prompts with these files:\n380\t  <name>: <path>\n381\t  ...one line per prompt override...\n382\t```\n383\t\n384\tThe `and replaces prompts ...` header and its lines appear only when there\n385\tare prompt overrides. The summary is printed even when no approval is\n386\tneeded (`roast trust`, 2.7 step 3).\n387\t\n388\t### 2.10 Yes/no answer parsing\n389\t\n390\t`promptNoDefault(out, reader, question)`: write `<question> [y/N] ` (no\n391\tnewline), read one line up to `\\n` from the shared line reader\n392\t(`00-decisions.md`: one reader per process run across prompts). EOF\n393\twithout a newline still yields the partial line. Trim whitespace,\n394\tlowercase; consent only for `y` or `yes`. Empty input, EOF, `n`, `no`,\n395\t`yy`, `sure` -> decline.\n396\t\n397\t### 2.11 Terminal detection\n398\t\n399\t`interactiveTerminals(stdin, stderr)` is a module-level swappable function\n400\t(tests replace it). Default: true only when both streams are real TTYs\n401\t(`isTTY === true` on a `tty.ReadStream` / `tty.WriteStream`); any\n402\tnon-file stream (string-backed test stream, pipe) is false.\n403\t\n404\t### 2.12 Stored credential files"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29c699d6-6603-42f0-a998-14114bd781a8","uuid":"8b163cae-392f-414a-84fe-3fc6d77c9af3","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:29:07.328Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-07-trust-hash-v2-and-trust-command/.scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/03-trust-credentials.md","content":"## 2. Behavior contract\n\n### 2.1 Built-in provider registry (consumed, not owned)\n\nOwned by the config area; restated because trust and credentials depend on\nit exactly.\n\n```\nname        endpoint                      protocols        key variable\nopenai      https://api.openai.com/v1     responses, chat  OPENAI_API_KEY\nanthropic   https://api.anthropic.com/v1  anthropic        ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\nopenrouter  https://openrouter.ai/api/v1  chat, responses  OPENROUTER_API_KEY\n```\n\nProtocols are listed in registry order (the first is the starter's).\n\n- `lookupProvider(name)`: exact, case-sensitive name match.\n- `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`: the provider that accepts `protocol`\n  and whose endpoint is the same endpoint as `endpoint`. At most one\n  matches.\n- `sameEndpoint(a, b)`: trim both, parse as URLs; false if either fails to\n  parse or has an empty host. Equal when scheme (case-insensitive), host\n  (case-insensitive, including port), path with trailing `/` removed,\n  raw query string, and userinfo all match. So `https://api.openai.com/v1/`\n  is the OpenAI endpoint.\n- A profile's endpoint is its `endpoint` field verbatim when set, else the\n  protocol default: `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` for `anthropic`,\n  `https://api.openai.com/v1` for every other protocol. OpenRouter is never\n  an implicit default.\n- A profile's protocol after `config.load` defaults to `responses` when\n  omitted. The projection always sees the defaulted protocol.\n- Author-route family keys are normalized by `config.load` (trimmed,\n  lowercased, canonical family). The projection sees normalized keys.\n\n### 2.2 Trust projection\n\n`trustProjection(cfg)` produces:\n\n```ts\ninterface TrustedProfile {\n    selector:string      // \"roles.finder\" or\n                         // \"roles.finder.author_routes.<family>\"\n    profile:string       // profile name\n    endpoint:string      // resolved endpoint string (2.1), not normalized\n    protocol:string      // defaulted protocol\n    credentialEnv:string // see below; \"\" when none\n}\ninterface PromptOverride {\n    name:string          // \"prompts.finder_code\" | \"prompts.finder_prose\"\n                         // | \"prompts.verifier\"\n    path:string          // raw string from the config, unresolved\n}\ninterface TrustProjection {\n    profiles:TrustedProfile[]\n    promptOverrides:PromptOverride[]\n}\n```\n\nOrder and contents:\n\n1. For each role name in `cfg.roles`, sorted ascending (in practice\n   `finder`, then `verifier`; `config.load` rejects any other role name):\n   1. the role's `profile`, selector `roles.<role>`;\n   2. then each author route, families sorted ascending, selector\n      `roles.<role>.author_routes.<family>`.\n   A selected profile name not present in `inference_profiles` is skipped\n   (unreachable after `config.load`, which rejects unknown references).\n   Duplicates are kept: the same profile selected twice appears twice.\n   Profiles no role or route selects are not in the projection.\n2. `credentialEnv` of a profile: its `api_key_env` when non-empty; else,\n   when `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` finds a provider, that provider's\n   key variable; else `\"\"`. The role override variable\n   (`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`) is never part of the projection.\n3. Prompt overrides in fixed order `finder_code`, `finder_prose`,\n   `verifier`; included only when the raw config string is non-empty. The\n   path is the raw string from `.roast.json` (not resolved against the\n   config directory), so approvals survive moving the worktree.\n\nThe projection is computed from the loaded config only. Command-line role\noverrides (`--<role>-base-url`, `--<role>-protocol`, ...) are not part of\nit: they are the operator's own choice on the command line, not the\nrepository's.\n\n### 2.3 Which configs need approval\n\n`requiresApproval(projection)` is true when:\n\n- `promptOverrides` is non-empty, or\n- any profile is not *standard*. A profile is standard when\n  `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` finds a provider `P` and\n  `credentialEnv === P.keyEnv`.\n\nConsequences (pinned by tests, 5.1): an implicit endpoint with its\nprotocol's provider is standard; a trailing slash on a built-in endpoint is\nstandard; OpenRouter with no `api_key_env` is standard (its `credentialEnv`\nbecomes `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`); any other key variable on a built-in\nendpoint (`MY_KEY`, another provider's key) needs approval; a protocol the\nprovider does not accept at that endpoint (Anthropic endpoint with `chat`,\nOpenAI endpoint with `anthropic`) needs approval; any custom endpoint needs\napproval, with or without a key variable.\n\n### 2.4 Trust hash v2 (deliberate change)\n\nThe approval hash identifies the projection's security-relevant values.\nSelectors and profile names are excluded: which approved destination a\nrole uses, or what a profile is called, does not change where code and\ncredentials go.\n\n**Hash document.** Build this JSON value from the projection:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"destinations\": [\n    {\"credential_env\": \"<string>\", \"endpoint\": \"<string>\",\n     \"protocol\": \"<string>\"}\n  ],\n  \"prompts\": [\n    {\"name\": \"<string>\", \"path\": \"<string>\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `destinations`: one entry per profile in the projection, with\n  `endpoint`, `protocol`, `credential_env` copied verbatim (no URL\n  normalization, no trimming). Remove exact duplicates (all three fields\n  equal). Sort ascending by `endpoint`, then `protocol`, then\n  `credential_env`, comparing each string by UTF-16 code units (the\n  JavaScript `<` operator on strings; the same order RFC 8785 uses for\n  keys). Always an array; `[]` when empty.\n- `prompts`: one entry per prompt override, in the projection's fixed\n  order (`prompts.finder_code`, `prompts.finder_prose`,\n  `prompts.verifier`), `name` including the `prompts.` prefix, `path`\n  verbatim. Not deduplicated, not sorted. Always an array; `[]` when empty.\n- No other members. No version field inside the document.\n\n**Canonicalization.** Serialize the document as RFC 8785 (JCS) canonical\nJSON. Because the document contains only objects, arrays, and strings,\nJCS reduces to: object members sorted by key in UTF-16 code-unit order; no\nwhitespace anywhere; arrays in the order given above; strings serialized\nexactly as ECMAScript `JSON.stringify` serializes a string (escape `\"`,\n`\\`, and U+0000..U+001F using `\\b \\t \\n \\f \\r` or `\\u00xx` lowercase hex;\neverything else, including `/` and non-ASCII, emitted literally). Key\norder for the two object kinds is therefore\n`credential_env, endpoint, protocol` and `name, path`, and the top level\nis `destinations, prompts`. Implement it in-house (about 20 lines); no new\ndependency.\n\n**Digest.** SHA-256 over the UTF-8 bytes of the canonical string. The\nstored and compared form is the string `sha256:` followed by the 64\nlowercase hex digits of the digest.\n\n**Known-answer vectors** (frozen; use as unit-test fixtures):\n\n1. One custom destination and one prompt override.\n   Canonical:\n   `{\"destinations\":[{\"credential_env\":\"GATEWAY_KEY\",\"endpoint\":\"https://gateway.example/v1\",\"protocol\":\"chat\"}],\"prompts\":[{\"name\":\"prompts.finder_code\",\"path\":\"finder-prompt.md\"}]}`\n   Hash:\n   `sha256:2b18773b1f654fbbd02019247674005c4035f3972cfc8552655a09bb2ac57d27`\n2. Two destinations (sorted), no prompts.\n   Canonical:\n   `{\"destinations\":[{\"credential_env\":\"OPENAI_API_KEY\",\"endpoint\":\"https://api.openai.com/v1\",\"protocol\":\"responses\"},{\"credential_env\":\"\",\"endpoint\":\"https://gw.example/v1\",\"protocol\":\"chat\"}],\"prompts\":[]}`\n   Hash:\n   `sha256:7ac3826edbb00b7b8294e6117b0fa3c70fd4e994de107bf41a23324761252d5c`\n\n(Lines above exceed 80 columns because the vectors must be exact.)\n\nProperties that follow and must hold:\n\n- Editing a model, a threshold, a limit, a gate setting, a profile name,\n  or which role points at an already-listed destination keeps the hash.\n- Changing any endpoint, protocol, credential variable, or prompt-override\n  path (including the routed profile's) changes the hash.\n- JSON key order and whitespace in `.roast.json` never affect the hash.\n\n### 2.5 State directory and trust record location\n\n- `stateDir(dir)`: run `git rev-parse --path-format=absolute\n  --git-common-dir` with working directory `dir`; trim the output; result\n  is `<output>/roast`. If git fails (not a repository), the error message\n  is `not inside a git repository`.\n- `trustPath(dir)` is `<stateDir(dir)>/trust.json`; on failure the error is\n  `trust: not inside a git repository`.\n- Linked worktrees resolve to the main repository's common dir, so they\n  share one record.\n- Every trust operation from the CLI passes the Git toplevel of the\n  process working directory (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`), not the\n  directory of a `--config` file.\n\n### 2.6 Trust record file (`trust.json`)\n\nVersion 2 shape, written by the TypeScript implementation:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 2,\n  \"approved\": [\n    \"sha256:2b18773b1f654fbbd02019247674005c4035f3972cfc8552655a09bb2ac57d27\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- Serialized with 2-space indentation, member order `version`, `approved`,\n  and a trailing newline (`JSON.stringify(rec, null, 2) + \"\\n\"`).\n- `approved` lists hashes in the order they were approved; no duplicates.\n- There is no `untrust` command. Deleting the file withdraws every approval\n  for the clone.\n\n**Reading** (`readTrustRecord(path)`) yields one of:\n\n| file state | result |\n|---|---|\n| absent (`ENOENT`) | `{kind:'current', approved:[]}` |\n| JSON object, `version` is the number `2` | `{kind:'current', approved}` |\n| JSON object, `version` is the number `1` | `{kind:'legacy'}` |\n| anything else | error |\n\n- For version 2: `approved` missing or `null` reads as `[]`; otherwise it\n  must be an array of strings, else error. Unknown top-level members are\n  ignored on read (Go parity) and dropped on the next write.\n- For version 1 (written by the Go implementation): nothing else in the\n  file is read or validated. Its hashes are never compared: the Go hash\n  serialization is not reproduced (ADR-0003).\n- Errors: unreadable file -> `read trust record: <cause>`; invalid JSON or\n  a non-object or wrongly typed `approved` -> `read trust record <path>:\n  <cause>`; `version` missing or any value other than 1 or 2 -> `trust\n  record <path> has unsupported version <v>`. Errors are never treated as\n  \"not approved\"; they propagate (2.8).\n\n**Approved check** (`isApproved(dir, hash)`) returns `'approved'`,\n`'not-approved'`, or `'legacy'` (record is version 1), or throws.\n\n**Approving** (`approve(dir, hash)`):\n\n1. Read the record.\n2. Version 2 with `hash` already present: return without writing.\n3. Version 2 otherwise: append `hash`, write.\n4. Version 1 (legacy): replace the whole file with a version 2 record\n   whose `approved` is `[hash]`. Legacy content is discarded, not\n   migrated.\n5. Absent: write a version 2 record `[hash]`.\n6. A read error (malformed, unsupported version) aborts approval; the file\n   is left untouched and the error propagates (Go parity: the user removes\n   or fixes the file).\n\n**Write procedure** (shared atomic write, 2.12): create the parent\ndirectory with `mkdir -p` mode `0o755` (subject to umask; an existing\ndirectory's mode is not changed), write a temp file `trust-<random>.json`\nin that directory opened with mode `0o600` and exclusive create, close,\nrename over `trust.json`, and remove the temp file on any failure. The\nresulting `trust.json` has mode `0o600`. Error prefix on any step:\n`write trust record: <cause>`.\n\n### 2.7 `roast trust` command\n\n```\nroast trust [--config <path>] [--yes]\n```\n\n- No positional arguments (extra args are a usage error, exit 2).\n- `--config <path>`: project config file; default is the nearest\n  `.roast.json` between the working directory and the Git toplevel\n  (config area's `loadProjectConfig`). Help text:\n  `project config file (default: nearest .roast.json)`.\n- `--yes`: record approval without asking; stdin is never read.\n- Short help: `Approve the project config's endpoints and credentials`.\n\nSteps, in order:\n\n1. Resolve the Git toplevel of the working directory. Failure -> usage\n   error `not inside a git repository` (exit 2).\n2. Load the project config (missing config -> the config area's usage\n   error naming `npx roast init`; invalid config -> usage error; both\n   exit 2).\n3. Compute the projection and print the trust summary (2.9) to **stdout**.\n4. If `!requiresApproval`: print `Only default provider endpoints and key\n   variables are used; no approval is needed.` to stdout, exit 0. Nothing\n   is written; no state directory is created.\n5. If not `--yes`: ask `Trust this project config? [y/N] ` on **stdout**\n   and read one answer from stdin (2.10). Anything but consent -> usage\n   error `not approved; nothing recorded` (exit 2), nothing written. A\n   stdin read error other than EOF -> that error (exit 4).\n6. `approve(toplevel, hash)`. Failure -> that error (exit 4).\n7. Print `Approved for this clone.` to stdout, exit 0.\n\nThe command prompts even when the hash is already approved (approval is\nidempotent). It does not check whether stdin is a terminal: with\nnon-terminal stdin it reads whatever is there, and EOF is a decline.\n\n### 2.8 `requireTrust` during `review`\n\n`requireTrust(dir, cfg, io)` runs inside `review` (including the default\nbare `roast` command) after the config is loaded, both roles are resolved\n(which may read stored credentials), role flag overrides and the finder\nflip are applied and validated, and the Git toplevel is found; and before\nthe review scope is built and before any network request.\n`dir` is the Git toplevel of the working directory.\n\n1. Compute the projection. If `!requiresApproval` -> continue (nothing\n   read or written).\n2. `isApproved(dir, hash)`:\n   - throws -> the review fails with that error. It is not a usage error,\n     so the exit code is 4 (Go parity: `ExitPartial`).\n   - `'approved'` -> continue.\n   - `'not-approved'` or `'legacy'` -> step 3.\n3. If stdin and stderr are not both terminals (`interactiveTerminals`,\n   2.11): fail with a usage error (exit 2), never reading stdin.\n   - not-approved message: `the project config names endpoints, credential\n     variables, or prompt files that are not approved for this clone;\n     review and approve them with `npx roast trust``\n   - legacy message (deliberate change): states that this clone's\n     approvals were recorded by an older Roast and are no longer\n     accepted, and names `npx roast trust` to approve again. Wording is\n     free; it must name `npx roast trust`.\n4. On terminals: for `'legacy'`, first print one line to stderr saying the\n   existing approvals came from an older Roast. Then print the trust\n   summary (2.9) to **stderr**, ask `Trust this project config? [y/N] `\n   on stderr, read one answer from stdin.\n   - Declined -> usage error `project config not approved; nothing\n     recorded and nothing sent` (exit 2), nothing written.\n   - stdin read error other than EOF -> that error (exit 4).\n   - Consent -> `approve(dir, hash)` (rewrites a legacy file as version\n     2); failure -> that error (exit 4); then print `Approved for this\n     clone.` to stderr and continue the review.\n\nUntrusted is never `ExitFindings` (1), so the pre-commit gate, which\nblocks only on exit 1, warns and allows the commit (gate area).\n\nThere is no environment-variable bypass of trust. CI approves with\n`npx roast trust --yes`.\n\n### 2.9 Trust summary format\n\nShared by `roast trust` (stdout) and the inline prompt (stderr). Every\ninterpolated value passes through `textsafe.line` (escapes C0 controls\nincluding newline and tab, DEL, C1 controls, invalid UTF-8; textsafe\narea), so config text cannot add lines or terminal escapes.\n\n```\nThis project config sends code and credentials to:\n  <selector>: profile <profile>\n    endpoint:   <endpoint>\n    protocol:   <protocol>\n    credential: <credentialEnv or \"(none named)\">\n  ...one 4-line block per projection profile, in projection order...\nand replaces prompts with these files:\n  <name>: <path>\n  ...one line per prompt override...\n```\n\nThe `and replaces prompts ...` header and its lines appear only when there\nare prompt overrides. The summary is printed even when no approval is\nneeded (`roast trust`, 2.7 step 3).\n\n### 2.10 Yes/no answer parsing\n\n`promptNoDefault(out, reader, question)`: write `<question> [y/N] ` (no\nnewline), read one line up to `\\n` from the shared line reader\n(`00-decisions.md`: one reader per process run across prompts). EOF\nwithout a newline still yields the partial line. Trim whitespace,\nlowercase; consent only for `y` or `yes`. Empty input, EOF, `n`, `no`,\n`yy`, `sure` -> decline.\n\n### 2.11 Terminal detection\n\n`interactiveTerminals(stdin, stderr)` is a module-level swappable function\n(tests replace it). Default: true only when both streams are real TTYs\n(`isTTY === true` on a `tty.ReadStream` / `tty.WriteStream`); any\nnon-file stream (string-backed test stream, pipe) is false.\n\n### 2.12 Stored credential files","numLines":370,"startLine":35,"totalLines":911}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016txEtu3h5CE919yKAUK7NL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChB2SLWg2HEHUjshyhrJd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KXDt6ABkXBaX6SkqtxrMU3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-07-trust-hash-v2-and-trust-command/.scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/03-trust-credentials.md","offset":560,"limit":260},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7429,"cache_read_input_tokens":65606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7429},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29c699d6-6603-42f0-a998-14114bd781a8","uuid":"2c471343-ca0f-485d-aad9-3dfba7e0a00c","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:29:09.578Z","request_id":"req_011ChB2SLJGYWZ2MNE3riqss","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KXDt6ABkXBaX6SkqtxrMU3":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-07-trust-hash-v2-and-trust-command/.scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/03-trust-credentials.md","offset":560,"limit":260}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KXDt6ABkXBaX6SkqtxrMU3","type":"tool_result","content":"560\t### 2.15 Security invariants enforced here\n561\t\n562\tFrom `docs/security-model.md`, ADR-0001, ADR-0002:\n563\t\n564\t1. No network request is made for a config that requires approval until\n565\t   this clone has approved its exact projection hash (review fails closed;\n566\t   doctor skips probes).\n567\t2. Approval state lives only under the Git common directory, which `git\n568\t   clone` never copies; a repository cannot ship its own approval.\n569\t3. Only `y` / `yes` records approval; `--yes` is the only non-interactive\n570\t   path. There is no environment-variable bypass.\n571\t4. Every value shown for approval is escaped, so config text cannot alter\n572\t   what the user is asked to approve.\n573\t5. Any change to an endpoint, protocol, credential variable, or\n574\t   prompt-override path invalidates the approval; other edits do not.\n575\t6. Approvals recorded by the Go implementation are never honored.\n576\t7. A malformed or unsupported trust record is an error, never read as\n577\t   \"approved\" or silently replaced.\n578\t8. A project config never holds a credential (config area rejects\n579\t   `api_key`); stored keys come only from credentials files outside the\n580\t   working tree.\n581\t9. A stored key is matched to a role only through the built-in provider\n582\t   registry (endpoint plus accepted protocol), so a custom endpoint, or a\n583\t   role an override moved off its provider's endpoint, never receives one.\n584\t10. Stored keys rank below every environment variable.\n585\t11. Credentials files are created `0o600` in a `0o700` directory; a file\n586\t    with any group/other permission bit stops resolution with an error\n587\t    naming `chmod 600`, never skipped.\n588\t12. A malformed credentials file (unknown provider or field, wrong\n589\t    version, empty key) is an error, never skipped.\n590\t13. Stored keys never appear in stdout, stderr, debug output, or run\n591\t    records (record area enforces exclusion from metadata; this area never\n592\t    logs a key or includes it in an error message).\n593\t\n594\t## 3. TypeScript module design\n595\t\n596\t```\n597\tsrc/config/trust.ts       projection, requiresApproval, hash v2\n598\tsrc/config/canonical.ts   RFC 8785 serializer for string-only documents\n599\tsrc/trust/trust.ts        stateDir, trustPath, record read/approve\n600\tsrc/credentials/credentials.ts\n601\t                          paths, load/save/delete, CredentialStore\n602\tsrc/fsutil/atomic.ts      writeFileAtomic (shared with trust; other areas\n603\t                          may reuse)\n604\tsrc/cli/trust.ts          `trust` command, requireTrust, summary, prompt\n605\tsrc/cli/prompt.ts         interactiveTerminals, line reader (shared)\n606\t```\n607\t\n608\t`src/config/trust.ts` (pure, no I/O):\n609\t\n610\t```ts\n611\texport interface TrustedProfile { /* 2.2 */ }\n612\texport interface PromptOverride { /* 2.2 */ }\n613\texport interface TrustProjection { /* 2.2 */ }\n614\texport function trustProjection(cfg:Config):TrustProjection\n615\texport function requiresApproval(p:TrustProjection):boolean\n616\texport function trustHashDocument(p:TrustProjection):HashDocument\n617\texport function trustHash(p:TrustProjection):string  // \"sha256:<hex>\"\n618\t```\n619\t\n620\t`trustHash` uses `node:crypto` `createHash('sha256')`. Depends on the\n621\tconfig area's `Config`, `providerAt`.\n622\t\n623\t`src/config/canonical.ts`:\n624\t\n625\t```ts\n626\texport type CanonicalValue =\n627\t    string|CanonicalValue[]|{[key:string]:CanonicalValue}\n628\texport function canonicalJson(value:CanonicalValue):string\n629\t```\n630\t\n631\tThrows on a string containing a lone surrogate (RFC 8785 requires\n632\twell-formed Unicode). This is a defensive backstop only: config\n633\tvalidation rejects lone surrogates (config area, spec.md cross-cutting\n634\trules), so hashing never sees one.\n635\t\n636\t`src/config/config.ts` (config area) exports the `StoredCredentials`\n637\tinterface (2.13) so config never imports `src/credentials/` (Go keeps the\n638\tsame direction: credentials imports config for `lookupProvider`).\n639\t\n640\t`src/trust/trust.ts`:\n641\t\n642\t```ts\n643\texport async function stateDir(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n644\t    :Promise<string>\n645\texport async function trustPath(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n646\t    :Promise<string>\n647\texport type TrustRecord =\n648\t    {kind:'current', approved:string[]}|{kind:'legacy'}\n649\texport async function readTrustRecord(path:string):Promise<TrustRecord>\n650\texport type ApprovalState = 'approved'|'not-approved'|'legacy'\n651\texport async function isApproved(dir:string, hash:string)\n652\t    :Promise<ApprovalState>\n653\texport async function approve(dir:string, hash:string):Promise<void>\n654\t```\n655\t\n656\tGit runs through `execFile('git', [...], {cwd:dir})` (Git area helper).\n657\t\n658\t`src/credentials/credentials.ts`:\n659\t\n660\t```ts\n661\texport const CREDENTIALS_FILE = 'credentials.json'\n662\texport type CredentialScope = 'project'|'user'\n663\texport interface Entry { key:string, storedAt:string }\n664\texport async function projectCredentialsPath(dir:string):Promise<string>\n665\texport function userCredentialsPath(\n666\t    env:Record<string, string|undefined>):string\n667\texport async function loadCredentials(path:string)\n668\t    :Promise<Map<string, Entry>>\n669\texport async function saveCredential(path:string, provider:string,\n670\t    key:string, storedAt:Date):Promise<void>\n671\texport async function deleteCredential(path:string, provider:string)\n672\t    :Promise<void>\n673\texport class CredentialStore implements StoredCredentials {\n674\t    constructor(dir:string, env:Record<string, string|undefined>)\n675\t    lookup(provider:string)\n676\t        :Promise<{key:string, scope:CredentialScope}|null>\n677\t}\n678\t```\n679\t\n680\t`storedAt` is kept as the original string so rewrites preserve it. The\n681\tdecoder is a `zod` strict schema plus the provider/key/version checks of\n682\t2.12. Depends on `src/config` (`lookupProvider`), `src/trust`\n683\t(`stateDir`), `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`.\n684\t\n685\t`src/fsutil/atomic.ts`:\n686\t\n687\t```ts\n688\texport async function writeFileAtomic(path:string, data:string, opts:{\n689\t    tempPrefix:string     // 'trust-' | 'credentials-'\n690\t    dirMode:number        // 0o755 | 0o700\n691\t    chmodDir:boolean      // false for trust, true for credentials\n692\t    errorPrefix:string    // 'write trust record' | 'write credentials'\n693\t}):Promise<void>\n694\t```\n695\t\n696\tTemp name: `<tempPrefix><random hex>.json`, opened with flags `wx` and\n697\tmode `0o600`.\n698\t\n699\t`src/cli/trust.ts`:\n700\t\n701\t```ts\n702\texport async function requireTrust(dir:string, cfg:Config,\n703\t    io:CliIo):Promise<void>        // throws UsageError or Error\n704\texport function writeTrustSummary(out:Writable,\n705\t    p:TrustProjection):void\n706\texport async function promptNoDefault(out:Writable,\n707\t    lines:LineReader, question:string):Promise<boolean>\n708\texport function trustCommand(...):CommandModule  // yargs\n709\t```\n710\t\n711\t`CliIo` is the in-process `run(argv, io)` bundle (stdin, stdout, stderr,\n712\tenv, cwd, shared line reader). `UsageError` maps to exit 2; other errors\n713\tto exit 4 (cli area). Debug namespace `roast:trust` and\n714\t`roast:credentials`: may log paths, scope names, provider names, record\n715\tversions, and hashes; never a key.\n716\t\n717\t## 4. Deliberate changes\n718\t\n719\t- **Trust hash v2** (decision 4, ADR-0003): SHA-256 over RFC 8785\n720\t  canonical JSON of the hash document in 2.4, `trust.json` `version: 2`.\n721\t  A version-1 record is never an approval: review fails as untrusted\n722\t  (exit 2, message says approved by an older Roast, names\n723\t  `npx roast trust`; on terminals the normal inline trust prompt is\n724\t  offered, as for any untrusted config); approving\n725\t  rewrites the file as version 2 with only the new hash. Differences from\n726\t  the Go hash document beyond the algorithm: empty arrays serialize as\n727\t  `[]` (Go wrote `null`), destination sort uses UTF-16 code units per\n728\t  field instead of bytes of NUL-joined fields. The stored form stays\n729\t  `sha256:<lowercase hex>`, and the test vectors in 2.4 are frozen.\n730\t- **Credentials keys are case-sensitive** (deliberate change 7): Go\n731\t  matched `credentials.json` field names case-insensitively\n732\t  (`{\"Version\":1,\"Providers\":...}` loaded); TS matches exactly, so such a\n733\t  file is rejected as an unknown member. Files Roast wrote are unaffected.\n734\t- **Windows dropped** (decision 2): the Go `runtime.GOOS != \"windows\"`\n735\t  guard on the permission check disappears; the check always runs.\n736\t- **Debug tracing** (decision 5): `roast:trust`, `roast:credentials`; no\n737\t  keys.\n738\t\n739\t## 5. Test plan\n740\t\n741\tSeams: CLI in-process (`run(argv, io)` with a `node:http` fake inference\n742\tserver and swappable `fetch`) unless noted. Every CLI test runs in a temp\n743\tGit repository and isolates `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in the injected\n744\tenv. Assert exit codes, files, and requests received; never exact text,\n745\texcept that a message names `npx roast trust` (behavioral contract).\n746\t\n747\t### 5.1 Projection and approval need (unit, `src/config/trust.ts`)\n748\t\n749\t- Table of single-profile configs and whether they need approval: OpenAI\n750\t  responses/chat with standard or no key var; implicit endpoint; implicit\n751\t  protocol; trailing slash; Anthropic; OpenRouter chat/responses with\n752\t  standard or no key; custom key var on each provider; another provider's\n753\t  key var; mismatched protocol; gateway with and without key var\n754\t  (TestStandardProfilesNeedNoApproval).\n755\t- Projection order: roles sorted, author routes sorted after their role;\n756\t  unselected profiles excluded; prompt overrides in fixed order (new).\n757\t\n758\t### 5.2 Hash v2 (unit)\n759\t\n760\t- Known-answer vectors 1 and 2 from 2.4 (new).\n761\t- Duplicate destinations collapse; destination order independent of role\n762\t  order; profile renames and selector changes keep the hash (new).\n763\t- `canonicalJson` matches RFC 8785 on key ordering and string escaping,\n764\t  including a non-ASCII key and a control character (new).\n765\t- Destinations sort field by field in UTF-16 code unit order (a case with\n766\t  an astral character versus a BMP character above U+E000); empty lists\n767\t  hash as `[]` (new).\n768\t- `canonicalJson` throws on a lone surrogate; the config area rejects one\n769\t  before hashing (new).\n770\t\n771\t### 5.3 Trust record (unit, `src/trust/trust.ts`, temp Git repo)\n772\t\n773\t- Absent file: not approved; approve writes version 2 with one hash, mode\n774\t  `0o600` (new).\n775\t- Approve twice: one entry (new).\n776\t- Version 1 file: `isApproved` returns `'legacy'`; approve replaces it\n777\t  with version 2 `[hash]` (new).\n778\t- Malformed JSON, `version: 3`, missing version, non-array `approved`:\n779\t  `isApproved` and `approve` both throw; file unchanged (new).\n780\t- Linked worktree resolves the same path (covered at CLI below).\n781\t\n782\t### 5.4 Review and the trust command (CLI)\n783\t\n784\t- Built-in-only config with author route reviews with no trust state\n785\t  written (TestDefaultEndpointConfigNeedsNoApproval).\n786\t- Untrusted config: exit neither 0 nor 1, message names `npx roast trust`,\n787\t  zero requests (TestUntrustedConfigFailsClosedBeforeAnyRequest).\n788\t- Summary names every profile, endpoint, protocol, credential var, prompt\n789\t  path, and asks `[y/N]` (TestTrustSummaryNamesEverySecurityRelevantValue;\n790\t  assert presence of config values only).\n791\t- Control characters in profile names, route families, and prompt paths\n792\t  never reach stdout raw; no config value starts its own line\n793\t  (TestTrustSummaryEscapesControlCharacters).\n794\t- `trust` records only on `y`/`Y`/`yes`/`y` without newline; `\"\"`, `\\n`,\n795\t  `n`, `no`, `yy`, `sure` exit non-zero, record nothing, and the next\n796\t  review still fails closed (TestTrustRecordsApprovalOnlyOnYes).\n797\t- `trust --yes` never reads stdin and enables the review\n798\t  (TestTrustYesRecordsWithoutReadingStdin).\n799\t- Model, threshold, limit, and gate edits keep approval\n800\t  (TestNonSecurityEditsKeepApproval).\n801\t- Endpoint, routed endpoint, protocol, credential var, and prompt path\n802\t  edits require re-approval, and re-approval works\n803\t  (TestSecurityEditsRequireApprovalAgain).\n804\t- Approval in the main worktree is honored in a linked worktree, and not\n805\t  before (TestApprovalIsSharedWithLinkedWorktrees).\n806\t- Inline prompt on simulated terminals: `y` approves, summary on stderr,\n807\t  record written, later non-terminal review passes\n808\t  (TestInlineTrustPromptApprovesOnYes).\n809\t- Inline decline (`\"\"`, `\\n`, `n`, `no`, `yy`): asks `[y/N]`, no requests,\n810\t  no record (TestInlineTrustPromptDeclinedSendsNothing).\n811\t- Non-terminal review checks for terminals, never reads stdin, never\n812\t  prompts, names `npx roast trust`\n813\t  (TestNonTerminalReviewFailsClosedWithoutPrompting).\n814\t- Default terminal detection rejects non-TTY streams\n815\t  (TestDefaultTerminalDetectionRejectsNonFiles; unit).\n816\t- Legacy record: review exits 2 with zero requests; `trust --yes` then\n817\t  makes it pass and the file is version 2; on simulated terminals `y`\n818\t  approves inline (new).\n819\t- Malformed record: review exits 4 with zero requests (new, Go parity;"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29c699d6-6603-42f0-a998-14114bd781a8","uuid":"8a476c18-2bd7-4708-a1ae-fa5d2a2058fa","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:29:09.598Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-07-trust-hash-v2-and-trust-command/.scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/03-trust-credentials.md","content":"### 2.15 Security invariants enforced here\n\nFrom `docs/security-model.md`, ADR-0001, ADR-0002:\n\n1. No network request is made for a config that requires approval until\n   this clone has approved its exact projection hash (review fails closed;\n   doctor skips probes).\n2. Approval state lives only under the Git common directory, which `git\n   clone` never copies; a repository cannot ship its own approval.\n3. Only `y` / `yes` records approval; `--yes` is the only non-interactive\n   path. There is no environment-variable bypass.\n4. Every value shown for approval is escaped, so config text cannot alter\n   what the user is asked to approve.\n5. Any change to an endpoint, protocol, credential variable, or\n   prompt-override path invalidates the approval; other edits do not.\n6. Approvals recorded by the Go implementation are never honored.\n7. A malformed or unsupported trust record is an error, never read as\n   \"approved\" or silently replaced.\n8. A project config never holds a credential (config area rejects\n   `api_key`); stored keys come only from credentials files outside the\n   working tree.\n9. A stored key is matched to a role only through the built-in provider\n   registry (endpoint plus accepted protocol), so a custom endpoint, or a\n   role an override moved off its provider's endpoint, never receives one.\n10. Stored keys rank below every environment variable.\n11. Credentials files are created `0o600` in a `0o700` directory; a file\n    with any group/other permission bit stops resolution with an error\n    naming `chmod 600`, never skipped.\n12. A malformed credentials file (unknown provider or field, wrong\n    version, empty key) is an error, never skipped.\n13. Stored keys never appear in stdout, stderr, debug output, or run\n    records (record area enforces exclusion from metadata; this area never\n    logs a key or includes it in an error message).\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\n```\nsrc/config/trust.ts       projection, requiresApproval, hash v2\nsrc/config/canonical.ts   RFC 8785 serializer for string-only documents\nsrc/trust/trust.ts        stateDir, trustPath, record read/approve\nsrc/credentials/credentials.ts\n                          paths, load/save/delete, CredentialStore\nsrc/fsutil/atomic.ts      writeFileAtomic (shared with trust; other areas\n                          may reuse)\nsrc/cli/trust.ts          `trust` command, requireTrust, summary, prompt\nsrc/cli/prompt.ts         interactiveTerminals, line reader (shared)\n```\n\n`src/config/trust.ts` (pure, no I/O):\n\n```ts\nexport interface TrustedProfile { /* 2.2 */ }\nexport interface PromptOverride { /* 2.2 */ }\nexport interface TrustProjection { /* 2.2 */ }\nexport function trustProjection(cfg:Config):TrustProjection\nexport function requiresApproval(p:TrustProjection):boolean\nexport function trustHashDocument(p:TrustProjection):HashDocument\nexport function trustHash(p:TrustProjection):string  // \"sha256:<hex>\"\n```\n\n`trustHash` uses `node:crypto` `createHash('sha256')`. Depends on the\nconfig area's `Config`, `providerAt`.\n\n`src/config/canonical.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport type CanonicalValue =\n    string|CanonicalValue[]|{[key:string]:CanonicalValue}\nexport function canonicalJson(value:CanonicalValue):string\n```\n\nThrows on a string containing a lone surrogate (RFC 8785 requires\nwell-formed Unicode). This is a defensive backstop only: config\nvalidation rejects lone surrogates (config area, spec.md cross-cutting\nrules), so hashing never sees one.\n\n`src/config/config.ts` (config area) exports the `StoredCredentials`\ninterface (2.13) so config never imports `src/credentials/` (Go keeps the\nsame direction: credentials imports config for `lookupProvider`).\n\n`src/trust/trust.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport async function stateDir(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n    :Promise<string>\nexport async function trustPath(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n    :Promise<string>\nexport type TrustRecord =\n    {kind:'current', approved:string[]}|{kind:'legacy'}\nexport async function readTrustRecord(path:string):Promise<TrustRecord>\nexport type ApprovalState = 'approved'|'not-approved'|'legacy'\nexport async function isApproved(dir:string, hash:string)\n    :Promise<ApprovalState>\nexport async function approve(dir:string, hash:string):Promise<void>\n```\n\nGit runs through `execFile('git', [...], {cwd:dir})` (Git area helper).\n\n`src/credentials/credentials.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport const CREDENTIALS_FILE = 'credentials.json'\nexport type CredentialScope = 'project'|'user'\nexport interface Entry { key:string, storedAt:string }\nexport async function projectCredentialsPath(dir:string):Promise<string>\nexport function userCredentialsPath(\n    env:Record<string, string|undefined>):string\nexport async function loadCredentials(path:string)\n    :Promise<Map<string, Entry>>\nexport async function saveCredential(path:string, provider:string,\n    key:string, storedAt:Date):Promise<void>\nexport async function deleteCredential(path:string, provider:string)\n    :Promise<void>\nexport class CredentialStore implements StoredCredentials {\n    constructor(dir:string, env:Record<string, string|undefined>)\n    lookup(provider:string)\n        :Promise<{key:string, scope:CredentialScope}|null>\n}\n```\n\n`storedAt` is kept as the original string so rewrites preserve it. The\ndecoder is a `zod` strict schema plus the provider/key/version checks of\n2.12. Depends on `src/config` (`lookupProvider`), `src/trust`\n(`stateDir`), `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`.\n\n`src/fsutil/atomic.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport async function writeFileAtomic(path:string, data:string, opts:{\n    tempPrefix:string     // 'trust-' | 'credentials-'\n    dirMode:number        // 0o755 | 0o700\n    chmodDir:boolean      // false for trust, true for credentials\n    errorPrefix:string    // 'write trust record' | 'write credentials'\n}):Promise<void>\n```\n\nTemp name: `<tempPrefix><random hex>.json`, opened with flags `wx` and\nmode `0o600`.\n\n`src/cli/trust.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport async function requireTrust(dir:string, cfg:Config,\n    io:CliIo):Promise<void>        // throws UsageError or Error\nexport function writeTrustSummary(out:Writable,\n    p:TrustProjection):void\nexport async function promptNoDefault(out:Writable,\n    lines:LineReader, question:string):Promise<boolean>\nexport function trustCommand(...):CommandModule  // yargs\n```\n\n`CliIo` is the in-process `run(argv, io)` bundle (stdin, stdout, stderr,\nenv, cwd, shared line reader). `UsageError` maps to exit 2; other errors\nto exit 4 (cli area). Debug namespace `roast:trust` and\n`roast:credentials`: may log paths, scope names, provider names, record\nversions, and hashes; never a key.\n\n## 4. Deliberate changes\n\n- **Trust hash v2** (decision 4, ADR-0003): SHA-256 over RFC 8785\n  canonical JSON of the hash document in 2.4, `trust.json` `version: 2`.\n  A version-1 record is never an approval: review fails as untrusted\n  (exit 2, message says approved by an older Roast, names\n  `npx roast trust`; on terminals the normal inline trust prompt is\n  offered, as for any untrusted config); approving\n  rewrites the file as version 2 with only the new hash. Differences from\n  the Go hash document beyond the algorithm: empty arrays serialize as\n  `[]` (Go wrote `null`), destination sort uses UTF-16 code units per\n  field instead of bytes of NUL-joined fields. The stored form stays\n  `sha256:<lowercase hex>`, and the test vectors in 2.4 are frozen.\n- **Credentials keys are case-sensitive** (deliberate change 7): Go\n  matched `credentials.json` field names case-insensitively\n  (`{\"Version\":1,\"Providers\":...}` loaded); TS matches exactly, so such a\n  file is rejected as an unknown member. Files Roast wrote are unaffected.\n- **Windows dropped** (decision 2): the Go `runtime.GOOS != \"windows\"`\n  guard on the permission check disappears; the check always runs.\n- **Debug tracing** (decision 5): `roast:trust`, `roast:credentials`; no\n  keys.\n\n## 5. Test plan\n\nSeams: CLI in-process (`run(argv, io)` with a `node:http` fake inference\nserver and swappable `fetch`) unless noted. Every CLI test runs in a temp\nGit repository and isolates `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in the injected\nenv. Assert exit codes, files, and requests received; never exact text,\nexcept that a message names `npx roast trust` (behavioral contract).\n\n### 5.1 Projection and approval need (unit, `src/config/trust.ts`)\n\n- Table of single-profile configs and whether they need approval: OpenAI\n  responses/chat with standard or no key var; implicit endpoint; implicit\n  protocol; trailing slash; Anthropic; OpenRouter chat/responses with\n  standard or no key; custom key var on each provider; another provider's\n  key var; mismatched protocol; gateway with and without key var\n  (TestStandardProfilesNeedNoApproval).\n- Projection order: roles sorted, author routes sorted after their role;\n  unselected profiles excluded; prompt overrides in fixed order (new).\n\n### 5.2 Hash v2 (unit)\n\n- Known-answer vectors 1 and 2 from 2.4 (new).\n- Duplicate destinations collapse; destination order independent of role\n  order; profile renames and selector changes keep the hash (new).\n- `canonicalJson` matches RFC 8785 on key ordering and string escaping,\n  including a non-ASCII key and a control character (new).\n- Destinations sort field by field in UTF-16 code unit order (a case with\n  an astral character versus a BMP character above U+E000); empty lists\n  hash as `[]` (new).\n- `canonicalJson` throws on a lone surrogate; the config area rejects one\n  before hashing (new).\n\n### 5.3 Trust record (unit, `src/trust/trust.ts`, temp Git repo)\n\n- Absent file: not approved; approve writes version 2 with one hash, mode\n  `0o600` (new).\n- Approve twice: one entry (new).\n- Version 1 file: `isApproved` returns `'legacy'`; approve replaces it\n  with version 2 `[hash]` (new).\n- Malformed JSON, `version: 3`, missing version, non-array `approved`:\n  `isApproved` and `approve` both throw; file unchanged (new).\n- Linked worktree resolves the same path (covered at CLI below).\n\n### 5.4 Review and the trust command (CLI)\n\n- Built-in-only config with author route reviews with no trust state\n  written (TestDefaultEndpointConfigNeedsNoApproval).\n- Untrusted config: exit neither 0 nor 1, message names `npx roast trust`,\n  zero requests (TestUntrustedConfigFailsClosedBeforeAnyRequest).\n- Summary names every profile, endpoint, protocol, credential var, prompt\n  path, and asks `[y/N]` (TestTrustSummaryNamesEverySecurityRelevantValue;\n  assert presence of config values only).\n- Control characters in profile names, route families, and prompt paths\n  never reach stdout raw; no config value starts its own line\n  (TestTrustSummaryEscapesControlCharacters).\n- `trust` records only on `y`/`Y`/`yes`/`y` without newline; `\"\"`, `\\n`,\n  `n`, `no`, `yy`, `sure` exit non-zero, record nothing, and the next\n  review still fails closed (TestTrustRecordsApprovalOnlyOnYes).\n- `trust --yes` never reads stdin and enables the review\n  (TestTrustYesRecordsWithoutReadingStdin).\n- Model, threshold, limit, and gate edits keep approval\n  (TestNonSecurityEditsKeepApproval).\n- Endpoint, routed endpoint, protocol, credential var, and prompt path\n  edits require re-approval, and re-approval works\n  (TestSecurityEditsRequireApprovalAgain).\n- Approval in the main worktree is honored in a linked worktree, and not\n  before (TestApprovalIsSharedWithLinkedWorktrees).\n- Inline prompt on simulated terminals: `y` approves, summary on stderr,\n  record written, later non-terminal review passes\n  (TestInlineTrustPromptApprovesOnYes).\n- Inline decline (`\"\"`, `\\n`, `n`, `no`, `yy`): asks `[y/N]`, no requests,\n  no record (TestInlineTrustPromptDeclinedSendsNothing).\n- Non-terminal review checks for terminals, never reads stdin, never\n  prompts, names `npx roast trust`\n  (TestNonTerminalReviewFailsClosedWithoutPrompting).\n- Default terminal detection rejects non-TTY streams\n  (TestDefaultTerminalDetectionRejectsNonFiles; unit).\n- Legacy record: review exits 2 with zero requests; `trust --yes` then\n  makes it pass and the file is version 2; on simulated terminals `y`\n  approves inline (new).\n- Malformed record: review exits 4 with zero requests (new, Go parity;","numLines":260,"startLine":560,"totalLines":911}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KXDt6ABkXBaX6SkqtxrMU3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
